<title>DENMARK: Chase Investment Bank sees higher Danish 1997 GDP.</title>
<headline>Chase Investment Bank sees higher Danish 1997 GDP.</headline>
<dateline>COPENHAGEN 1997-07-14</dateline>
<text>
<p>In its latest survey U.S. Chase Investment Bank on Monday raised its forecast for Denmark's 1997  GDP (gross domestic product) growth to 2.8 percent from 2.6 percent in its last forecast in February.</p>
<p>At the same time it left its estimate for Denmark's expected inflation rate this year unchanged at 2.4 percent.</p>
<p>The latest Danish Economy Ministry forecast in May foresaw 3.3 percent GDP growth, 2.3 percent inflation, 7.8 percent unemployment and a current account surplus of 12.0 billion crowns for this year.</p>
<p>The following estimates were made by 10 leading local and international organisations in recent months.</p>
<p>	  (pct)	 GDP    inflation  unemployment c/a (bln Dkk)</p>
<p>Chase (7/97)	  2.8    2.4	   --	    --</p>
<p>Codan Bank (7/97)   3.3    2.1	  7.7	   14.0</p>
<p>Svenska HB (7/97)   3.5    2.1	  7.8	   10.0</p>
<p>Goldman Sachs (7/97)3.3    2.1	  7.8	    1.8</p>
<p>M Stanley (7/97)    3.5    2.0	  8.0	   11.5</p>
<p>Nykredit (7/97)     2.6    2.1	  8.2	   10.0</p>
<p>Jyske Bank (6/97)   3.2    2.2	  7.9	   10.0</p>
<p>JP Morgan (6/97)    3.4    2.2	  7.8	   10.7</p>
<p>Barclays (6/97)     3.0    2.3	  7.8	    9.8</p>
<p>M Stanley (6/97)    3.5    2.1	  8.0	    --</p>
<p>--Chris Follett, Copenhagen newsroom +45-33969652</p>
